WEBVTT

title-1
00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:01.580
Loopback Transformer:

title-2
00:00:01.580 --> 00:00:04.890
towards a unified view of
residual looped Transformers.

hook-3
00:00:07.000 --> 00:00:10.670
Looped Transformers reduce
parameters by reusing shared blocks,

hook-4
00:00:10.940 --> 00:00:14.810
but sharing alone does not decide
how repeated computation is reused.

analysis-5
00:00:16.900 --> 00:00:20.510
Addition lets the total
update weight grow with depth,

analysis-6
00:00:20.520 --> 00:00:25.050
and a joint softmax makes the
input compete with the updates.

method-7
00:00:27.450 --> 00:00:30.060
Loopback stores each block's update,

method-8
00:00:30.290 --> 00:00:32.560
keeps the input on its own path,

method-9
00:00:32.850 --> 00:00:37.240
and lets decay set the total weight
while attention allocates it.

scale-10
00:00:39.250 --> 00:00:42.960
From 191M to 3B parameters,

scale-11
00:00:43.370 --> 00:00:45.535
Loopback reaches lower loss and a higher

scale-12
00:00:45.535 --> 00:00:48.350
thirteen-task average than
the standard Transformer,

scale-13
00:00:48.350 --> 00:00:51.780
with 40–75% of its parameters.

nine-14
00:00:54.550 --> 00:00:58.720
Against the standard Transformer
and seven looped residual designs,

nine-15
00:00:59.110 --> 00:01:01.684
Loopback has the lowest
loss and the highest

nine-16
00:01:01.684 --> 00:01:04.700
thirteen-task average at
all three scales tested.

math-17
00:01:07.700 --> 00:01:08.850
At larger scale,

math-18
00:01:08.920 --> 00:01:11.970
it also predicts math and
code solutions better,

math-19
00:01:12.200 --> 00:01:13.830
and at 3B,

math-20
00:01:14.200 --> 00:01:19.010
word-problem accuracy
rises from 2.6% to 6.4%.

visits-21
00:01:21.750 --> 00:01:24.840
Run more loops at
inference than in training,

visits-22
00:01:25.210 --> 00:01:28.220
and Loopback degrades far
less than plain addition.

ablation-23
00:01:30.950 --> 00:01:32.240
Both controls matter:

ablation-24
00:01:32.390 --> 00:01:34.380
without attention the loss rises,

ablation-25
00:01:34.710 --> 00:01:36.680
without decay it rises much more,

ablation-26
00:01:37.030 --> 00:01:39.160
and the gains hold across initializations.

depth-27
00:01:41.500 --> 00:01:43.090
At a fixed parameter budget,

depth-28
00:01:43.580 --> 00:01:46.570
deeper and narrower standard
Transformers lose ground,

depth-29
00:01:46.780 --> 00:01:49.010
while Loopback's margin grows with depth.

eff-30
00:01:51.200 --> 00:01:54.790
The read adds under 0.1% in FLOPs;

eff-31
00:01:55.060 --> 00:01:56.130
at 3B,

eff-32
00:01:56.820 --> 00:02:00.770
training runs within 6% of the
standard Transformer's speed.

outro-33
00:02:02.650 --> 00:02:04.180
Loopback stores updates,

outro-34
00:02:04.590 --> 00:02:06.700
keeps the input on its own path,

outro-35
00:02:07.050 --> 00:02:09.740
and lets decay and attention
control their weight:

outro-36
00:02:10.210 --> 00:02:12.100
lower loss with fewer parameters.
